steps.body:
A class is a blueprint for objects that share the same shape and behavior. Name it in PascalCase. The `constructor` runs once for every `new` call and stores the starting values on `this`, the object being built. Methods go in the class body with no `function` keyword and no commas between them.

```javascript
class Point {
  constructor(x, y) {
    this.x = x;
    this.y = y;
  }

  distanceFromOrigin() {
    return Math.hypot(this.x, this.y);
  }
}

const p = new Point(3, 4);
console.log(p.distanceFromOrigin()); // 5
```

`new Point(3, 4)` creates an instance. Inside a method, `this` is the instance the method was called on, so `p.distanceFromOrigin()` reads `p.x` and `p.y`. Always write `this.x` inside methods: a bare `x` is not the field.

Watch out for these:

- Classes are not hoisted like function declarations. Like `let`, using `Point` above its declaration throws a ReferenceError, so declare classes first.
- Calling `Point(3, 4)` without `new` throws a TypeError.
- Class bodies always run in strict mode, so assigning to an undeclared name inside a method throws instead of quietly creating a global.
- A method pulled off its instance loses `this`. `const f = p.distanceFromOrigin;` then `f()` throws a TypeError, so call methods on the instance, or wrap them in an arrow such as `() => p.distanceFromOrigin()`. The next lesson shows more fixes.

example.code:
class Rectangle {
  constructor(width, height) {
    this.width = width;
    this.height = height;
  }

  area() {
    return this.width * this.height;
  }

  perimeter() {
    return 2 * (this.width + this.height);
  }
}

const small = new Rectangle(3, 4);
const square = new Rectangle(5, 5);

console.log(`${small.width} x ${small.height}: area ${small.area()}, perimeter ${small.perimeter()}`);
console.log(`${square.width} x ${square.height}: area ${square.area()}, perimeter ${square.perimeter()}`);

