class Circle {
  constructor(radius) {
    this.radius = radius;
  }

  area() {
    return Math.PI * this.radius ** 2;
  }

  circumference() {
    return 2 * Math.PI * this.radius;
  }
}

const coin = new Circle(1);
const plate = new Circle(2.5);

console.log(`Radius ${coin.radius}: area ${coin.area().toFixed(2)}, circumference ${coin.circumference().toFixed(2)}`);
console.log(`Radius ${plate.radius}: area ${plate.area().toFixed(2)}, circumference ${plate.circumference().toFixed(2)}`);

steps.body:
You can also declare fields right in the class body. `count = 0;` gives every new instance its own `count` starting at 0, set before the constructor body runs. Use a field for starting values that do not come from arguments.

```javascript
class Counter {
  count = 0;

  increment() {
    this.count++;
  }
}
```

Methods change state by assigning through `this`. Each instance keeps its own fields, so `a.increment()` never touches `b.count`.

A class guards its data by refusing bad changes. Check the request with an `if` and return early before changing anything:

```javascript
withdraw(amount) {
  if (amount > this.balance) {
    return false;
  }
  this.balance -= amount;
  return true;
}
```

Returning `true` or `false` tells the caller whether the change happened. Outside code can still write `account.balance = -5` directly. Private fields, covered in the next lesson, close that gap.

example.code:
class BankAccount {
  balance = 0;

  constructor(owner) {
    this.owner = owner;
  }

  deposit(amount) {
    if (amount <= 0) {
      return false;
    }
    this.balance += amount;
    return true;
  }

  withdraw(amount) {
    if (amount <= 0 || amount > this.balance) {
      return false;
    }
    this.balance -= amount;
    return true;
  }
}

function report(account, action, ok) {
  console.log(`${account.owner} ${action}: ${ok ? "ok" : "refused"}, balance ${account.balance}`);
}

const ada = new BankAccount("Ada");
const bo = new BankAccount("Bo");

report(ada, "deposit 100", ada.deposit(100));
report(ada, "withdraw 30", ada.withdraw(30));
report(ada, "withdraw 500", ada.withdraw(500));
report(bo, "deposit 50", bo.deposit(50));
report(bo, "deposit -20", bo.deposit(-20));
console.log(`Ada has ${ada.balance}, Bo has ${bo.balance}`);

